<script lang="ts">
	import { GraphStat, GraphTimer, GraphUptime, type UptimeStatus } from '$lib';

	const days: UptimeStatus[] = [
		'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'degraded', 'ok', 'ok',
		'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok',
		'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'degraded', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok',
		'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'down', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok',
		'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok',
		'ok', 'ok', 'degraded', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok'
	];
</script>

# Uptime pages are just text

Every status page is eventually the same artifact: a row of colored
squares, a percentage, a couple of timestamps. Somewhere along the way we
decided that implied a charting library, a canvas, and a client-side
renderer fed by a JSON endpoint. It never did. A status page is at its best
when it is text — legible in a terminal, diffable in a pull request,
readable with images turned off on a train.

Here is one: sixty days of a single service, drawn entirely from glyphs.

<GraphUptime title="API" days={days} from="JUL 01" to="AUG 29" />

Fifty-six green blocks. The single dot is August 4 — thirty-one minutes of
502s while a connection pool quietly exhausted itself. The three hatched
cells are mornings where p99 drifted past half a second and nobody got
paged over it.

Ninety-three percent reads worse than it lived. As a number it floats in a
void; as a grid you can see the shape of the two months, and the shape says
one bad afternoon.

<GraphTimer kind="ago" at="2026-08-04T14:09:00Z" caption="since the page-one incident" />

That timer is live. It counts away from a fixed timestamp and re-renders on
an interval, which is exactly right for the one number on a status page
people actually watch: how long since it last broke.

<GraphStat title="LAST 60 DAYS" items={[{ value: '93%', label: 'days green', accent: true }, { value: '3', label: 'slow mornings' }, { value: '31min', label: 'total downtime' }]} />

The headline figures, because an incident review asks for numbers before it
asks for a diagram. Three components, three imports, no chart library — the
whole surface is a file that looks like this:

```svelte
<GraphUptime
	title="API"
	days={[
		'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'degraded', 'ok', 'ok',
		// ...forty-three more days
		'ok', 'ok', 'degraded', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok', 'ok'
	]}
	from="JUL 01"
	to="AUG 29"
/>
```

The data model is an array of four words. If your monitor can emit
`'ok' | 'degraded' | 'down'`, it can publish a status page — no aggregation
pipeline, no screenshot of a dashboard bolted into a wiki.

There is a practical reason to prefer text here, beyond taste. Outages are
read under the worst conditions: a cramped phone screen, a spotty
connection, an impatient engineer scrolling one-handed. Text is the only
format that gets *lighter* under those conditions. It reflows, it copies
cleanly into an incident channel, and it survives being pasted into a
terminal without turning into a screenshot of itself.

This post is itself an .svx file. The three figures above are imports from
markgraphy, rendered in place — nothing is an image, and nothing needed
a client-side chart runtime to draw.

So if your status page is a canvas, you are shipping a renderer to draw
eleven characters.
